## Project-specific context

This project has two codelines, both TypeScript ESM projects using vitest:

- **mocka** (`/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-a`) — fare logic. Tests live in `test/*.test.ts`, source in `src/*.ts`. Test cases use `describe`/`it`/`expect` from `vitest`. The fare function `fareFor` takes a `Rider` (`{ age: number; hasPass: boolean }`) and returns a fare in cents. Constants `BASE_FARE_CENTS` (350) and `CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS` (175) are exported and used in assertions. The current bug (MOCK3-1) is an off-by-one: the code uses `rider.age > 65` instead of `>= 65`, so a rider aged exactly 65 is charged the adult fare instead of the concession fare. A test covering this requirement must assert that `fareFor({ age: 65, hasPass: false })` returns `CONCESSION_FARE_CENTS` — not merely that age 70 (well past the boundary) gets the concession fare, which would pass even with the buggy `>` comparison.

- **mockb** (`/home/bradleyjerome/projects/mock3/mock-b`) — departure board rendering. Tests live in `test/*.test.ts`, source in `src/*.ts`. The `formatStops` function takes a `Stop[]` (`{ name: string; minutes: number }`) and joins stop names with ` -> `. The current bug (MOCK3-2) is that the loop runs `i < stops.length - 1`, omitting the final stop. A test covering this requirement must assert that the formatted string contains the **last** stop's name — not merely that the first stop or the arrow separator is present, which would pass even with the omission bug.

Both projects run tests with `npx vitest run` (or `npm test`) and type-check with `npx tsc --noEmit`. Test files import directly from `../src/*` using ESM `import` syntax. TypeScript is in `strict` mode.
